PhotoCraft
----------

Portions of this project's image-adjustment mathematics are adapted from
PhotoCraft, an open-source, clean-room photo editor written in pure Rust.

    Project:  PhotoCraft
    Authors:  storytold / the ArtCraft Team and the PhotoCraft contributors
    Source:   https://github.com/storytold/photocraft
    License:  MIT OR Apache-2.0 (dual-licensed; see licenses/)

The full license texts are included in this repository:

    licenses/PhotoCraft-LICENSE-MIT.txt
    licenses/PhotoCraft-LICENSE-APACHE-2.0.txt

What PhotoCraft Express takes from PhotoCraft is image-adjustment and
filter mathematics — nothing else. Specifically:

  CONSULTED (read as the reference for how the operations should behave):
    - The overall adjustment model of the photocraft-compose crate
      (crates/compose/src/adjust.rs): Levels, Curves, Vibrance,
      Hue/Saturation, Exposure.
    - Hue/Saturation scaling semantics and the white-balance approach in
      the raw/develop code, used as background reading only.

  ADAPTED (PhotoCraft algorithms re-implemented in Kotlin in
  app/src/main/java/com/shawnrodis/photocraftexpress/engine/AdjustMath.kt,
  and used by engine/Looks.kt; each file carries a header noting this):
    - Levels midtone gamma, including its fitted shadow "toe"
      (PhotoCraft's `levels_gamma`), and the Levels transform structure
      of input stretch -> gamma -> output range (`levels_float`).
    - The Curves LUT built from a natural cubic spline through control
      points (PhotoCraft's `curve_lut`).
    - The Vibrance pixel model (PhotoCraft's `vibrance_lin`): the
      hue-preserving saturation change, its fitted boost coefficients,
      and its skin-tone damping by hue.
    - The Saturation grey weights 0.288 R + 0.712 G (from PhotoCraft's
      `vibrance_px`).

  Documented adaptations: PhotoCraft applies these operations in linear
  light through a document transfer curve and samples its LUTs at 4096
  entries for float pipelines. This app applies the same mathematics
  directly to sRGB-encoded values and samples LUTs at 256 entries for an
  8-bit mobile pipeline. Exposure is applied through a 2.2-gamma round
  trip inside the tone LUT. These choices change shadow weighting
  slightly; the algorithms' structure and fitted constants are
  PhotoCraft's.

Everything else in this repository — all Android code, the whole user
interface, the icon artwork, the crop/geometry engine, the undo history,
the MediaStore/export code, and all Look recipes — is original work
written for PhotoCraft Express.

